.TH TX\-ENVELOPE 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.3.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
tx\-envelope \- common signing envelope (public half). Reads one JSON object from stdin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
tx\-envelope [\-\-json OBJ]: common signing envelope (decode|verify|check|verify_thor_send|chain_table); public data only
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
(or `\-\-json '{...}'`) and prints one JSON object: decode / verify / check / verify_thor_send / chain_table
across UTXO, EVM, ZEC, Sol and Tron. Never takes a private key. Same schema as the C ABI
`bt_envelope` and `blocktools::envelope::run` (`envelope":"blocktools.sign/v1"`).
.nf
.RS 2
  echo '{"op":"decode","chain":"btc","tx":"<hex|psbt>"}' | tx\-envelope
  tx\-envelope \-\-json '{"op":"chain_table","chain":"_"}'
  tx\-envelope \-\-json '{"op":"verify_thor_send","chain":"ltc","tx":"<psbt>","vault":"ltc1...","memo":"=:...","amount":"20000000","change":"ltc1...","taker":["ltc1..."]}'
    THORChain inbound send check: result {ok, failures[], outputs, memo, fee, rbf}; failures name the
    reason (vault, amount, memo, change, output_count, rbf, fee, input_unknown, input_foreign, tx, chain).
.RE
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.nf
\-\-json
.fi
Each flag's meaning is in the SYNOPSIS usage line(s) above (taken from \-\-help).
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
tx\-envelope: common signing envelope (public half). Reads one JSON object from stdin
(or `\-\-json '{...}'`) and prints one JSON object: decode / verify / check / verify_thor_send / chain_table
.nf
.RS 2
  tx\-envelope \-\-json '{"op":"chain_table","chain":"_"}'
  tx\-envelope \-\-json '{"op":"verify_thor_send","chain":"ltc","tx":"<psbt>","vault":"ltc1...","memo":"=:...","amount":"20000000","change":"ltc1...","taker":["ltc1..."]}'
    THORChain inbound send check: result {ok, failures[], outputs, memo, fee, rbf}; failures name the
    reason (vault, amount, memo, change, output_count, rbf, fee, input_unknown, input_foreign, tx, chain).
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
last status ok
.TP
1
last status error
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ tx\-envelope \-\-json '{"op":"chain_table"}'
# exit 1, last status: error
$ tx\-envelope \-\-json '{"op":"verify_thor_send","chain":"ltc","tx":"zz"}'
# exit 0, last status: ok
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
It may open hostile wallet files and handle secrets: run it sealed and offline (./blocktools run; sealed\-run allows it).
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
